Platform sources

The upstream platform JSON files here are from TapiocaFox/Daijishou at
cde33bb64af2eb1cc56965c8a68702b3485a6d73, under the MIT license in LICENSE.

Commodore128.json and Atari800.json are local derivatives, not official
upstream platforms. That pinned upstream set has no dedicated C128 or Atari
8-bit computer platform. They derive from Commodore64.json and Atari5200.json,
respectively, with separate platform/player IDs and the matching core, so
computer games do not default to the C64 or 5200 emulator. The Atari computer
platform uses its own extensions and database; C128 has no configured scraper.
Daijishou cannot update these two local platforms from its official catalog.

New upstream files copied for the additional cores:
  ScummVM.json, VIC20.json, CommodorePlus4.json, CommodorePET.json,
  AmstradCPC.json, Quake.json, CaveStoryGameEngine.json, LowResNX.json,
  SharpX68000.json, AtariJaguar.json.

Phase 2 sources

Unmodified files copied from that same cached revision:
  AtariST.json, NECPCFX.json, NECPC98.json, NECPC88.json, TIC80.json, WASM4.json, Uzebox.json, WataraSupervision.json, FairchildChannelF.json, SharpX1.json, Arduboy.json, BBCMicro.json, MegaDuck.json, ZX81.json.

The following are local derivatives, NOT members of the official catalog. Each
has its own andr36oid.phase2 system and player IDs, no inherited scraper or
achievements, and the supported extensions from dArkOSen's core info. Their
system/folder names follow dArkOSen's EmulationStation systems where present.
CBM-II and BK have no dedicated entry in that reference ES config; the local
folders are named for the machines in the corresponding core info files.
  Vircon32.json <- LowResNX.json; folder vircon32; core vircon32.
  SuperCassetteVision.json <- ColecoVision.json; folder scv; core emuscv.
  Macintosh.json <- DOS.json; folder vmac; core minivmac.
  Wolfenstein3D.json <- DOOMGameEngine.json; folder wolf; core ecwolf.
  ONScripter.json <- RPGMaker.json; folder onscripter; core onsyuri.
  PuzzleScript.json <- LowResNX.json; folder puzzlescript; core puzzlescript.
  CommodoreCBM2.json <- Commodore64.json; folder cbm2; core vice_xcbm2.
  CommodoreCBM5x0.json <- Commodore64.json; folder cbm5x0; core vice_xcbm5x0.
  ElektronikaBK.json <- ZXSpectrum.json; folder bk; core bk.

The generator applies per-core filename filters for Phase 2 players. This fixes
missing extensions in cached player definitions, and prevents synthesized players
from inheriting another core's formats. The source JSON copies stay unchanged.
Macintosh is the upstream Mini vMac Mac II build, not a claim of a Mac Plus
variant. ECWolf uses native data extensions rather than ES shell launchers or
.wolf/.ecwolf wrappers; those Linux launch scripts do not work on Android.
Local platforms use the existing artwork fallback until host artwork regeneration.

Additional libraries reusing Phase 1/base cores:
  SegaSG1000.json and SegaPico.json are unchanged copies from the same cache.
  AmstradGX4000.json is a local derivative of AmstradCPC.json with its own
  andr36oid.phase2.gx4000 platform/player IDs and CPR cartridge player
  (plus RetroArch archive extraction). Caprice32's pinned source has CPR
  loading in cap32/cart.c. No new emulator binary is needed for these systems.
  The c16 and atarixegs folders join their existing plus4 and atari800 platforms
  to preserve dArkOSen card paths without duplicating a machine library.
